from itertools import permutations\nfrom itertools import chain\n\n# Using permutations from itertools to generate distinct permutations\n# (using ALL combinations of the digits '2', '9', '2', '9')\ndigits = '2299'\nperms = set(permutations(digits))\n# Convert the set back to a list for easy manipulation\nperms_list = list(perms)\n\n# Remove duplicate numbers by converting them back to a string\ndistinct_permutations = set(''.join(p) for p in perms_list)\n\n# The distinct_permutations list holds all unique permutations of \"2299\".\n# Convert it into a set first to get unique values (they should not be repeated)\nunique_numbers = set(distinct_permutations)\n# Breaking down each valid permutation into a flat list\nflat_list = [num for sublist in unique_numbers for num in sublist]\n# Calculate the total number of unique four-digit numbers\ntotal_possible_numbers = len(flat_list)\nprint(total_possible_numbers)\n\n\nout:\n5\n```\nThe number of unique four-digit integers that can be formed using the digits 2, 2, 9 and 9 is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
